;;; S-expression bus specs (draft)

;;; The S-expression bus has the following lines:

SBUS-DATA	32 bits, subdivided as follows:
		SBUS-TYPE	n bits
		SBUS-ADDRESS	32-n bits; also called SBUS-DATUM
SBUS-OP		5-bit operation code
SBUS-TRIMOS	3 lines for TRIMOSBUS sequencing
SBUS-BUSY	asserted by bus master; not asserted if no master
SBUS-GRANT	[daisy-chained or something]
???

While SBUS-BUSY is asserted, the bus master may initiate one or more
transactions.  Each transaction consists of one or more cycles, where
each cycle corresponds to one advancing of the TRIMOSBUS sequencer.
During a cycle, the sender asserts SBUS-DATA and SBUS-OP, and then
advances his sequencer.  Each other processors then examines the OP and DATA,
latches it if appropriate, and then advances its sequencer.  When all
processors have so advanced, the cycle has ended.

The bus master is not always the sender.  Who is to be the sender depends
on the OP values and associated protocols.  Typically on the first cycle
of a transaction the bus master sends out a request, possibly
followed by more cycles to transmit additional arguments.  For a broadcast
message stepping the bus constitutes acknowledgement.  For a message to
a particular respondent, the respondent normally sends at least one
return message, which may be a reply or an error signal.

Conventions for SBUS data.

The high two bits of the TYPE field describe the meaning of the data.  00
or 01 means that the ADDRESS field identifies a two-pointer cell, whose
two 32-bit halves are called the car and cdr.  This is called a list
pointer.  11 means that the DATUM field identifies no other object, but
is immediate data.  This is called an "inum pointer"; one type is called
fixnum.  10 means that the ADDRESS field identifies a set of 32-bit
pointer cells; the car operation on such a pointer yields a fixnum whose
DATUM field is the number of cells in the set.  This is an array pointer.


Transactions.

Each transaction takes one or more arguments from the master and then
optionally produces one result.  Some transactions may be responded to by
any processor; this is done by requiring every processor to examine the
first argument (passed when SBUS-REQ is asserted), which may not be an
inum pointer.  The address part determines which processor is to respond.
By convention all addresses with the high bit zero are reserved to the
on-chip storage allocator, and addresses with the high bit one to be
parceled out to other devices.

The possible operations are as follows (-> indicates a result,
and ? indicates any processor can respond):

? CAR(ARG) -> RESULT
	RESULT is the first half of ARG, which may not be an inum.

? CDR(ARG) -> RESULT
	RESULT is the second half of ARG, which may not be an inum.

? RPLACA(ARG,NEWLEFT)
	The left half of ARG1 is altered to be NEWRIGHT.
	ARG1 must be a list pointer.

? RPLACD(ARG,NEWRIGHT)
	The left half of ARG1 is altered to be NEWRIGHT.
	ARG1 must be a list pointer.

? AREF(ARRAY,INDEX) -> RESULT
	INDEX must be an inum whose DATUM is between zero (inclusive)
	and the length (car) of ARRAY (exclusive).  RESULT is the
	contents of the INDEX'th pointer cell of ARRAY, which must be
	an array pointer.

? ASET(ARRAY,INDEX,NEWVAL)
	INDEX must be an inum whose DATUM is between zero (inclusive)
	and the length (car) of ARRAY (exclusive).  The INDEX'th pointer
	cell of ARRAY, which must be an array pointer, is altered to be
	NEWVAL.

  CONS(ARG1,ARG2) -> RESULT
	A new pointer cell is allocated, the two halves initialized to
	ARG1 and ARG2, and a list pointer with type=0 is returned.
	This is always handled by the on-chip storage allocator.

  NCONS(ARG) -> RESULT
	This is equivalent to CONS(ARG,0) -> RESULT; that is, the
	right half of the allocated cell is set to zero (which
	is a list pointer to the empty list, of course).
	This is always handled by the on-chip storage allocator.

  ACONS(LENGTH) -> RESULT
	An array with LENGTH pointer cells is allocated, and a pointer
	to it with type 1000...0 is returned.  LENGTH must be an inum.
	This is always handled by the on-chip storage allocator.

? RELOCATE(ARG) -> RESULT
	This is used only during garbage collection.  ARG should
	be a pointer (not an inum) into the "old space" of whatever
	processor owns that address.  The processor should respond
	with the "new address" for that object.  This may entail
	copying that object first.  In the on-chip storage allocator,
	if the car of the object points into newspace, then that
	is the correct result; otherwise the object is copied.

  SET-MEMTOP(ARG)
	The MEMTOP register is initialized from ARG, which should be an
	inum indicating the size of (half of the) memory, in 64-bit
	pointer cells.
	This is always handled by the on-chip storage allocator.

  SET-ALLOC-PTR(ARG)
	The ALLOC-PTR register is initialized from ARG, which should be an
	inum indicating the place in memory, in 64-bit pointer cells.
	This is a very dangerous operation, normally used only when booting.
	This is always handled by the on-chip storage allocator.

  GET-ALLOC-PTR(ARG) -> RESULT
	The argument is ignored.  The result is an inum suitable for
	giving to SET-ALLOC-PTR.

[That's 13 operations, so unless more are added that's 4 bits for SBUS-OP.]


Sneaky efficiency trick.

The SBUS has three more lines that only the on-chip evaluator can use:
SBUS-EVAL	This request is from the on-chip evaluator.
		The full acknowledgement protocol is unnecessary.
		Any result will be placed in EVAL-PRIVATE-REG.
SBUS-NORET	Do not return the result on the SBUS.
SBUS-ELIDE	Take the last argument for this operation from
		EVAL-PRIVATE-REG rather than the SBUS.


Error returns.

If the operation fails for some reason, an error code is returned
in the OP field of the SBUS.  Some of the error codes are:

INUM-NOT-ALLOWED
INUM-REQUIRED
ARRAY-REQUIRED
LIST-REQUIRED
INVALID-ARRAY-SIZE
INDEX-OUT-OF-RANGE
NON-EXISTENT-MEMORY (probably asserted after a long timeout)
WRITE-INTO-READ-ONLY ?
